            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 53,
              "start": 53
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Mitchell, missouri, the petitioner, asks the Court to reverse the Missouri trial court\u2019s decision and to apply the Elstad framework, arguing that the failure to give Miranda warnings for the first statement should not automatically taint a later warned statement."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Irving L. Gornstein",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "303372bb99f1d08ecc67139b5772a8b9f612b46fb86d786aee8c9c8c2e299fcb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-303372bb99f1-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 18,
              "start": 18
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Irving L. Gornstein, the United States, filing as amicus curiae, supports Missouri\u2019s position, stating that an officer\u2019s failure to give Miranda warnings before an initial statement does not presumptively taint a subsequent statement that is preceded by proper warnings and a waiver."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Amy M. Barthlow",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "303372bb99f1d08ecc67139b5772a8b9f612b46fb86d786aee8c9c8c2e299fcb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-303372bb99f1-0001-0001",
              "ev-303372bb99f1-0001-0002",
              "ev-303372bb99f1-0001-0003",
              "ev-303372bb99f1-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 33,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Amy M. Barthlow, patrice\u202fSeibert, the respondent, argues that systematic interrogation and the risk of coercion raise serious concerns about the voluntariness of the second confession, emphasizing that the tactic undermines free will."
      }
    ],
